export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"addon":"electrical","name":"Electrical","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"electrical-start","scope":"addon:electrical","title":"Electrical: getting started","summary":"What the Electrical add-on does in CadCraft, where its tools are, and how a schematic is kept in an ordinary DWG or DXF file.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["electrical","add-on","schematic","wiring diagram","installation plan","iec 60617","ansi","jic","nfpa 79","elsymbol","elwire"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"your-first-schematic","text":"Your first schematic","level":2},{"id":"files-open-anywhere","text":"Files open anywhere","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EElectrical is an add-on for CadCraft that comes with it and is free. It does two jobs:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESchematics\u003C/strong\u003E: control and power circuits drawn with standard symbols, wires that know what they connect, wire numbers, device tags, cross-references between a coil and its contacts, numbered sheets, terminal plans, cable lists and a parts list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EInstallation plans\u003C/strong\u003E: outlets, switches and luminaires placed on a building plan, each on a circuit, the load of every circuit, cable lengths measured along the routes you draw, and a panel schedule.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESwitch it on or off in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E. Its tools are in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElectrical\u003C/span\u003E group at the end of the toolbar (with a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu), in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElectrical\u003C/span\u003E section of the right panel, and as commands that all start with \u003Ccode\u003EEL\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-first-schematic\"\u003EYour first schematic\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELSHEET\u003C/code\u003E, choose \u003Ccode\u003EA3\u003C/code\u003E and give the sheet a title. A sheet opens with a frame, a title block and ten numbered paths across the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELSYMBOL\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EELS\u003C/code\u003E), choose a group and a symbol, and click where it goes. A coil gets its tag from the path it is on, for example \u003Ccode\u003E-K102\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELWIRE\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EELW\u003C/code\u003E) and click from one connection to the next. A wire ends by itself when it reaches a connection or another wire.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPlace a contact and give it the coil&#039;s tag: the coil lists it under itself and the contact says where its coil is.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELNUMBER\u003C/code\u003E to number the wires, and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELCHECK\u003C/code\u003E to find anything left open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/addon:electrical/electrical-schematics\" data-topic=\"addon:electrical/electrical-schematics\"\u003EDraw a schematic\u003C/a\u003E for the whole walkthrough.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-open-anywhere\"\u003EFiles open anywhere\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything Electrical draws is ordinary CadCraft content: symbols are blocks with attributes, wires are lines and polylines on layers that start with \u003Ccode\u003EEL-WIRE\u003C/code\u003E, sheets are layouts with a frame block. So a schematic saved as DWG or DXF opens in any CAD program, and opens in CadCraft without the add-on, with nothing lost. Switching the add-on on again brings back the cross-references and checks.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EKeep wires on layers named \u003Ccode\u003EEL-WIRE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEL-WIRE-24VDC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEL-WIRE-L1\u003C/code\u003E and so on: any layer whose name starts with \u003Ccode\u003EEL-WIRE\u003C/code\u003E holds wires.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What it is Electrical is an add-on for CadCraft that comes with it and is free. It does two jobs: Schematics: control and power circuits drawn with standard symbols, wires that know what they connect, wire numbers, device tags, cross-references between a coil and its contacts, numbered sheets, terminal plans, cable lists and a parts list. Installation plans: outlets, switches and luminaires placed on a building plan, each on a circuit, the load of every circuit, cable lengths measured along the routes you draw, and a panel schedule. Switch it on or off in Settings › General › Add-ons. Its tools are in the Electrical group at the end of the toolbar (with a More menu), in the Electrical section of the right panel, and as commands that all start with EL. Your first schematic Type ELSHEET, choose A3 and give the sheet a title. A sheet opens with a frame, a title block and ten numbered paths across the top. Type ELSYMBOL (or ELS), choose a group and a symbol, and click where it goes. A coil gets its tag from the path it is on, for example -K102. Type ELWIRE (or ELW) and click from one connection to the next. A wire ends by itself when it reaches a connection or another wire. Place a contact and give it the coil's tag: the coil lists it under itself and the contact says where its coil is. Type ELNUMBER to number the wires, and ELCHECK to find anything left open. See Draw a schematic for the whole walkthrough. Files open anywhere Everything Electrical draws is ordinary CadCraft content: symbols are blocks with attributes, wires are lines and polylines on layers that start with EL-WIRE, sheets are layouts with a frame block. So a schematic saved as DWG or DXF opens in any CAD program, and opens in CadCraft without the add-on, with nothing lost. Switching the add-on on again brings back the cross-references and checks. Keep wires on layers named EL-WIRE, EL-WIRE-24VDC, EL-WIRE-L1 and so on: any layer whose name starts with EL-WIRE holds wires."},{"slug":"electrical-schematics","scope":"addon:electrical","title":"Draw a schematic","summary":"Place symbols, draw wires between their connections, and let junctions, cross-references and tags follow as you work.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["electrical","schematic","symbol","elsymbol","els","elwire","elw","wire","junction","dot","contact","coil","connection","pin","rotate","terminal","elupdate","elcable","cable"],"headings":[{"id":"sheets-and-paths","text":"Sheets and paths","level":2},{"id":"place-symbols","text":"Place symbols","level":2},{"id":"draw-wires","text":"Draw wires","level":2},{"id":"cross-references","text":"Cross-references","level":2},{"id":"cables","text":"Cables","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"sheets-and-paths\"\u003ESheets and paths\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA schematic sheet (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELSHEET\u003C/code\u003E) is a layout with a frame. The numbers across the top and bottom are its \u003Cstrong\u003Epaths\u003C/strong\u003E: every symbol and wire is on a sheet and a path, written \u003Ccode\u003E2.5\u003C/code\u003E for sheet 2, path 5. Tags, wire numbers and cross-references are made from them. The title block&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlant\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E=\u003C/code\u003E) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELocation\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E+\u003C/code\u003E) apply to everything on the sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-symbols\"\u003EPlace symbols\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELSYMBOL\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EELS\u003C/code\u003E), or click a symbol in the right panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElectrical\u003C/span\u003E section.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose a group (contacts, switches, coils, protection, loads, terminals, PLC) and a symbol. You can also type a symbol&#039;s name at the first prompt, for example \u003Ccode\u003Ecoil\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Econtact.no\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E to turn it 90°, then click where it goes. On a sheet it lands on the 2.5 mm grid, so connections line up.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA contact asks for the tag of its coil. Coils, buttons, lamps, motors and protection devices get a tag at once (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/addon:electrical/electrical-numbers-tags\" data-topic=\"addon:electrical/electrical-numbers-tags\"\u003EWire numbers and tags\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPlaced on a wire, a symbol cuts the wire under itself, so it sits in the circuit instead of being shorted by it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-wires\"\u003EDraw wires\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELWIRE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EELW\u003C/code\u003E) and click the first connection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the next points. With \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWires go straight across and down\u003C/span\u003E on (the default) a corner is added for you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe wire ends by itself when you click a connection or another wire; press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end it anywhere else. \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E takes the last point back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow wires connect:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA wire ending on a connection, on another wire&#039;s end or on another wire&#039;s middle is connected. Where three or more meet, a junction dot appears.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETwo wires that only cross are \u003Cstrong\u003Enot\u003C/strong\u003E connected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA terminal joins its top and bottom.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EJunction dots and cross-references update by themselves after every change, inside the same undo step. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELUPDATE\u003C/code\u003E does it on demand (for a drawing edited elsewhere, or with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate cross-references as you draw\u003C/span\u003E switched off).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cross-references\"\u003ECross-references\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything with one designation (\u003Ccode\u003E=A1+S1-K1\u003C/code\u003E) is one device. The coil lists its contacts beneath itself (\u003Ccode\u003ENO 13-14 /2.5\u003C/code\u003E: a make contact, connections 13-14, on sheet 2 path 5), and every contact says where its coil is (\u003Ccode\u003E/1.2\u003C/code\u003E). Move a contact to another sheet and both change. A coil shows eight lines; more contacts end with &quot;and n more&quot;, and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELCHECK\u003C/code\u003E says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cables\"\u003ECables\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELCABLE\u003C/code\u003E draws a cable mark across the wires it carries: click either side of the wires, then give the cable&#039;s tag (\u003Ccode\u003E-W1\u003C/code\u003E) and type. The cable list shows a core for every wire the mark crosses.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Sheets and paths A schematic sheet (ELSHEET) is a layout with a frame. The numbers across the top and bottom are its paths: every symbol and wire is on a sheet and a path, written 2.5 for sheet 2, path 5. Tags, wire numbers and cross-references are made from them. The title block's Plant (=) and Location (+) apply to everything on the sheet. Place symbols Type ELSYMBOL (ELS), or click a symbol in the right panel's Electrical section. Choose a group (contacts, switches, coils, protection, loads, terminals, PLC) and a symbol. You can also type a symbol's name at the first prompt, for example coil or contact.no. Type R to turn it 90°, then click where it goes. On a sheet it lands on the 2.5 mm grid, so connections line up. A contact asks for the tag of its coil. Coils, buttons, lamps, motors and protection devices get a tag at once (see Wire numbers and tags). Placed on a wire, a symbol cuts the wire under itself, so it sits in the circuit instead of being shorted by it. Draw wires Type ELWIRE (ELW) and click the first connection. Click the next points. With Wires go straight across and down on (the default) a corner is added for you. The wire ends by itself when you click a connection or another wire; press Enter to end it anywhere else. U takes the last point back. How wires connect: A wire ending on a connection, on another wire's end or on another wire's middle is connected. Where three or more meet, a junction dot appears. Two wires that only cross are not connected. A terminal joins its top and bottom. Junction dots and cross-references update by themselves after every change, inside the same undo step. ELUPDATE does it on demand (for a drawing edited elsewhere, or with Update cross-references as you draw switched off). Cross-references Everything with one designation (=A1+S1-K1) is one device. The coil lists its contacts beneath itself (NO 13-14 /2.5: a make contact, connections 13-14, on sheet 2 path 5), and every contact says where its coil is (/1.2). Move a contact to another sheet and both change. A coil shows eight lines; more contacts end with \"and n more\", and ELCHECK says so. Cables ELCABLE draws a cable mark across the wires it carries: click either side of the wires, then give the cable's tag (-W1) and type. The cable list shows a core for every wire the mark crosses."},{"slug":"electrical-numbers-tags","scope":"addon:electrical","title":"Wire numbers and tags","summary":"Number wires by sheet, path and sequence, tag devices in the IEC 81346 style, and keep the numbers you typed yourself.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["electrical","wire number","elnumber","eln","numbering","scheme","tag","eltag","iec 81346","designation","plant","location","device tag","lock","terminal number"],"headings":[{"id":"number-the-wires","text":"Number the wires","level":2},{"id":"tags","text":"Tags","level":2},{"id":"properties","text":"Properties","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"number-the-wires\"\u003ENumber the wires\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELNUMBER\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EELN\u003C/code\u003E) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Every connected net gets one number, placed beside its longest wire on its first sheet. Run it again after changes: numbers that are still right stay, new nets get new ones, and numbers left on no wire are removed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe scheme is set with \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E, or in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElectrical\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EScheme\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGives\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{sheet}.{path}.{seq}\u003C/code\u003E (default)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E2.4.1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2.4.2\u003C/code\u003E … on sheet 2, path 4\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{sheet}{path:02}-{seq}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E204-1\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EW{seq:03}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EW001\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EW002\u003C/code\u003E …\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E switches between one number per net and one number per wire.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo keep a number you typed, select the wire number and switch on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep this number\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E. A kept number is never given to another net.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tags\"\u003ETags\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA tag has three parts: the \u003Cstrong\u003Eplant\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E=A1\u003C/code\u003E), the \u003Cstrong\u003Elocation\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E+S1\u003C/code\u003E) and the \u003Cstrong\u003Edevice\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E-K3\u003C/code\u003E). The device part is the symbol&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETag\u003C/span\u003E; plant and location come from the sheet&#039;s title block unless the symbol has its own. The whole designation names one device: a coil and all its contacts, or the three poles of a breaker drawn apart.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENew coils, buttons, lamps, motors and protection devices are tagged when you place them (switch this off in Settings). \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELTAG\u003C/code\u003E tags everything still untagged; \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E tags everything again; \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E chooses the scheme:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eletter + sheet + path\u003C/strong\u003E (default): \u003Ccode\u003E-K204\u003C/code\u003E is a relay on sheet 2, path 4; a second one there is \u003Ccode\u003E-K204.2\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eletter + number\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E-K1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-K2\u003C/code\u003E …\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe letters follow IEC 81346-2: K relays and contactors, Q power switching, F protection, S switches and buttons, B sensors, M motors, P signals, T transformers, X terminals, A PLC. Contacts are never tagged automatically: they take their coil&#039;s tag. Terminals get the strip \u003Ccode\u003E-X1\u003C/code\u003E and the next free number.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"properties\"\u003EProperties\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect a symbol to see its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElectrical\u003C/span\u003E section in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E: tag, plant, location, function text, maker, part and order number, its full designation, where it is, and where its coil or contacts are. A wire shows its number and what it connects.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Number the wires Type ELNUMBER (ELN) and press Enter. Every connected net gets one number, placed beside its longest wire on its first sheet. Run it again after changes: numbers that are still right stay, new nets get new ones, and numbers left on no wire are removed. The scheme is set with F, or in Settings › CadCraft › Electrical: Scheme Gives {sheet}.{path}.{seq} (default) 2.4.1, 2.4.2 … on sheet 2, path 4 {sheet}{path:02}-{seq} 204-1 W{seq:03} W001, W002 … P switches between one number per net and one number per wire. To keep a number you typed, select the wire number and switch on Keep this number in Properties. A kept number is never given to another net. Tags A tag has three parts: the plant (=A1), the location (+S1) and the device (-K3). The device part is the symbol's Tag; plant and location come from the sheet's title block unless the symbol has its own. The whole designation names one device: a coil and all its contacts, or the three poles of a breaker drawn apart. New coils, buttons, lamps, motors and protection devices are tagged when you place them (switch this off in Settings). ELTAG tags everything still untagged; A tags everything again; S chooses the scheme: letter + sheet + path (default): -K204 is a relay on sheet 2, path 4; a second one there is -K204.2. letter + number: -K1, -K2 … The letters follow IEC 81346-2: K relays and contactors, Q power switching, F protection, S switches and buttons, B sensors, M motors, P signals, T transformers, X terminals, A PLC. Contacts are never tagged automatically: they take their coil's tag. Terminals get the strip -X1 and the next free number. Properties Select a symbol to see its Electrical section in Properties: tag, plant, location, function text, maker, part and order number, its full designation, where it is, and where its coil or contacts are. A wire shows its number and what it connects."},{"slug":"electrical-sheets-lists","scope":"addon:electrical","title":"Sheets, terminal plans and lists","summary":"Add numbered sheets, then make the sheet list, terminal plan, cable list and parts list as tables on new sheets or as CSV files.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["electrical","elsheet","sheet","frame","title block","path","elreport","elr","sheet list","terminal plan","terminal list","cable list","parts list","bill of materials","bom","csv","export"],"headings":[{"id":"sheets","text":"Sheets","level":2},{"id":"lists","text":"Lists","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"sheets\"\u003ESheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELSHEET\u003C/code\u003E adds a sheet (A3 with ten paths, or A4 with eight). A new sheet takes the project, plant, location and drawn-by values of the first sheet; the title is asked. The title block&#039;s fields are the frame&#039;s attributes: select the frame and change them in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E. Sheet numbers come from the title block&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheet\u003C/span\u003E field.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lists\"\u003ELists\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELREPORT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EELR\u003C/code\u003E) and choose:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EList\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it says\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E Sheet list\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eevery sheet: number, title, plant, location, revision, date\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E Terminal plan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eevery terminal by strip: its number, wire number, and what its top and its bottom connect to\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E Cable list\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eevery core of every marked cable: wire number, from, to, the cable&#039;s type and length\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E Parts list\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eone row per maker and part number, how many, and which tags use it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThen \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E puts it on new sheets (as many as it needs, &quot;Parts list (1 of 3)&quot; …), or \u003Ccode\u003EV\u003C/code\u003E saves a CSV file. The same four lists are in \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe parts list reads the maker, part number and order number of each device from the part that has them: the coil for a contactor and its contacts, the device for everything else. Terminals count one by one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA CSV cell that starts with \u003Ccode\u003E=\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E+\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E (a tag like \u003Ccode\u003E-K1\u003C/code\u003E) gets a leading \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;\u003C/code\u003E, so a spreadsheet shows it as text and never runs it as a formula.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"Sheets ELSHEET adds a sheet (A3 with ten paths, or A4 with eight). A new sheet takes the project, plant, location and drawn-by values of the first sheet; the title is asked. The title block's fields are the frame's attributes: select the frame and change them in Properties. Sheet numbers come from the title block's Sheet field. Lists Type ELREPORT (ELR) and choose: List What it says S Sheet list every sheet: number, title, plant, location, revision, date T Terminal plan every terminal by strip: its number, wire number, and what its top and its bottom connect to C Cable list every core of every marked cable: wire number, from, to, the cable's type and length P Parts list one row per maker and part number, how many, and which tags use it Then D puts it on new sheets (as many as it needs, \"Parts list (1 of 3)\" …), or V saves a CSV file. The same four lists are in EXPORT. The parts list reads the maker, part number and order number of each device from the part that has them: the coil for a contactor and its contacts, the device for everything else. Terminals count one by one. A CSV cell that starts with =, +, - or @ (a tag like -K1) gets a leading ', so a spreadsheet shows it as text and never runs it as a formula."},{"slug":"electrical-installation","scope":"addon:electrical","title":"Installation plans and the panel schedule","summary":"Place outlets, switches and luminaires on a plan, put them on circuits, measure cable lengths along routes, and balance the panel.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["electrical","installation","plan","socket","outlet","switch","luminaire","light","elinstall","eli","circuit","elcircuit","load","elroute","route","cable length","tray","elpanel","panel schedule","elbalance","phase","balance","board","breaker"],"headings":[{"id":"place-the-devices","text":"Place the devices","level":2},{"id":"circuits","text":"Circuits","level":2},{"id":"cable-lengths","text":"Cable lengths","level":2},{"id":"the-panel-schedule","text":"The panel schedule","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"place-the-devices\"\u003EPlace the devices\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the building plan (model space) and type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELINSTALL\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EELI\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose a group and a symbol: socket outlets (also data, telephone and television outlets), switches and sensors (key and time switches, thermostat), luminaires (also exit signs), detectors and alarms (smoke and heat detectors, manual call point, sounder), other (junction box, appliance, fan, water heater, cooker, EV charging point, energy meter), or a distribution board or sub-board.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive the circuit (for example \u003Ccode\u003EC3\u003C/code\u003E), then click each place; \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E turns the symbol. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E ends.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESymbols are drawn at the plan&#039;s scale: 1:50 for a plan in millimetres, or what you set in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElectrical\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlan symbol scale\u003C/span\u003E. Each symbol carries a planning load in watts (a socket 200 W, a luminaire 60 W …) that you change in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELibrary items placed in the drawing count too when they have electrical data (kilowatts, or amps and volts): put them on a circuit with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELCIRCUIT\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"circuits\"\u003ECircuits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELCIRCUIT\u003C/code\u003E with \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E puts selected devices on a circuit. With \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E it defines one: description, breaker rating, the residual-current device in front of it (for example \u003Ccode\u003ERCD1 30 mA A\u003C/code\u003E, or empty), phase (\u003Ccode\u003EL1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EL2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EL3\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EL1L2\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E3P\u003C/code\u003E) and board, written on a small label you place next to the board.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe RCD text is yours to word, but include the trip current in \u003Ccode\u003EmA\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELCHECK\u003C/code\u003E reads it and reminds you of a circuit that feeds socket outlets with no residual-current device of 30 mA or less. It is a planning reminder, not a check against any code: your local rules decide.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cable-lengths\"\u003ECable lengths\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDraw the routes cables take (trays, conduits, along walls) with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELROUTE\u003C/code\u003E, or on any layer whose name starts with \u003Ccode\u003EEL-ROUTE\u003C/code\u003E. A circuit&#039;s cable runs from its board along the shortest routes to each of its devices; a run two devices share is counted once. A device joins the routes when it is within half a metre of one. Half a metre is added at each device and a metre at the board.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-panel-schedule\"\u003EThe panel schedule\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELPANEL\u003C/code\u003E lists every circuit of every board: breaker, RCD, phase, load, current, cable and length, the load on each phase and how unbalanced they are. \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E prints it in the history, \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E puts it on a sheet, \u003Ccode\u003EV\u003C/code\u003E saves a CSV.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELBALANCE\u003C/code\u003E spreads the single-phase circuits over L1, L2 and L3, heaviest first onto the lightest phase. Two- and three-phase circuits stay where they are. One \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Place the devices Open the building plan (model space) and type ELINSTALL (ELI). Choose a group and a symbol: socket outlets (also data, telephone and television outlets), switches and sensors (key and time switches, thermostat), luminaires (also exit signs), detectors and alarms (smoke and heat detectors, manual call point, sounder), other (junction box, appliance, fan, water heater, cooker, EV charging point, energy meter), or a distribution board or sub-board. Give the circuit (for example C3), then click each place; R turns the symbol. Enter ends. Symbols are drawn at the plan's scale: 1:50 for a plan in millimetres, or what you set in Settings › CadCraft › Electrical › Plan symbol scale. Each symbol carries a planning load in watts (a socket 200 W, a luminaire 60 W …) that you change in Properties. Library items placed in the drawing count too when they have electrical data (kilowatts, or amps and volts): put them on a circuit with ELCIRCUIT. Circuits ELCIRCUIT with A puts selected devices on a circuit. With D it defines one: description, breaker rating, the residual-current device in front of it (for example RCD1 30 mA A, or empty), phase (L1, L2, L3, L1L2 or 3P) and board, written on a small label you place next to the board. The RCD text is yours to word, but include the trip current in mA: ELCHECK reads it and reminds you of a circuit that feeds socket outlets with no residual-current device of 30 mA or less. It is a planning reminder, not a check against any code: your local rules decide. Cable lengths Draw the routes cables take (trays, conduits, along walls) with ELROUTE, or on any layer whose name starts with EL-ROUTE. A circuit's cable runs from its board along the shortest routes to each of its devices; a run two devices share is counted once. A device joins the routes when it is within half a metre of one. Half a metre is added at each device and a metre at the board. The panel schedule ELPANEL lists every circuit of every board: breaker, RCD, phase, load, current, cable and length, the load on each phase and how unbalanced they are. L prints it in the history, D puts it on a sheet, V saves a CSV. ELBALANCE spreads the single-phase circuits over L1, L2 and L3, heaviest first onto the lightest phase. Two- and three-phase circuits stay where they are. One Ctrl+Z undoes it."},{"slug":"electrical-checks","scope":"addon:electrical","title":"Check a schematic","summary":"Find duplicate tags, open wire ends, contacts with no coil, shorted symbols and overloaded circuits, and go to each one.","section":"tasks","order":50,"keywords":["electrical","elcheck","elc","check","error","warning","duplicate tag","open end","unconnected","contact without coil","overload","breaker","circuit","cores"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELCHECK\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EELC\u003C/code\u003E). The history lists every finding, errors first, each with where it is. Type a finding&#039;s number to go to it (its sheet opens and its objects are selected); press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFinding\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eused twice\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etwo coils (or motors, lamps, PLC points) with one designation, or one terminal number twice in a strip\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPLC address used twice\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etwo PLC points with one address\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ehas no tag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea symbol that needs a tag has none; a contact without a tag is an error\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Econnected to nothing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea wire end that reaches no connection and no other wire\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eshorts it\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea wire runs through a symbol&#039;s connection instead of ending on it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Econtacts have no coil\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Econtacts carry a tag no coil has\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eno contact drawn\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea coil none of whose contacts is on a sheet (a warning)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emirror shows 8\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emore contact lines than the coil can list\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Edraws … on a … breaker\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea circuit&#039;s current is over its breaker rating (over 80 % is a warning)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eon no circuit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ean installation device without a circuit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eno breaker rating\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea circuit with loads and no defined breaker\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enames no residual-current device\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea circuit that feeds socket outlets and has no RCD of 30 mA or less in its RCD text (a warning; a planning reminder, not a code check)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emore wires than cores\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea cable mark crosses more wires than its cores\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eon no wire\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea wire number left where no wire is\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E","text":"Type ELCHECK (ELC). The history lists every finding, errors first, each with where it is. Type a finding's number to go to it (its sheet opens and its objects are selected); press Enter to end. Finding Means used twice two coils (or motors, lamps, PLC points) with one designation, or one terminal number twice in a strip PLC address used twice two PLC points with one address has no tag a symbol that needs a tag has none; a contact without a tag is an error connected to nothing a wire end that reaches no connection and no other wire shorts it a wire runs through a symbol's connection instead of ending on it contacts have no coil contacts carry a tag no coil has no contact drawn a coil none of whose contacts is on a sheet (a warning) mirror shows 8 more contact lines than the coil can list draws … on a … breaker a circuit's current is over its breaker rating (over 80 % is a warning) on no circuit an installation device without a circuit no breaker rating a circuit with loads and no defined breaker names no residual-current device a circuit that feeds socket outlets and has no RCD of 30 mA or less in its RCD text (a warning; a planning reminder, not a code check) more wires than cores a cable mark crosses more wires than its cores on no wire a wire number left where no wire is"},{"slug":"electrical-import-symbols","scope":"addon:electrical","title":"Use another symbol library","summary":"Turn the blocks of a DWG or DXF symbol library into Electrical symbols that tag, wire and list like the built-in ones.","section":"tasks","order":62,"keywords":["electrical","import","symbol library","elimportsym","blocks","dwg","dxf","attributes","terminals","connection points"],"headings":[{"id":"what-is-read-from-a-block","text":"What is read from a block","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EIf your symbols already live in a DWG or DXF drawing as blocks, you can bring them in instead of redrawing them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELIMPORTSYM\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EELIS\u003C/code\u003E) and choose the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003ESuggested types\u003C/strong\u003E to import every block whose name says what it is (\u003Ccode\u003ERELAY_COIL\u003C/code\u003E is a coil, \u003Ccode\u003EPB_NO\u003C/code\u003E a pushbutton, \u003Ccode\u003EFUSE_2A\u003C/code\u003E a fuse …), or \u003Cstrong\u003EOne by one\u003C/strong\u003E to say for each block which symbol type it is, or skip it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESay whether the library is drawn in millimetres or inches. Inch libraries are scaled to millimetres, the unit Electrical draws in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach block then acts as the type you chose: it gets a tag from the path grid, wires connect to it, coils carry the contact mirror and it shows up in the lists. Place them with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELSYMBOL\u003C/code\u003E › \u003Cstrong\u003EImported\u003C/strong\u003E. They are kept in this browser for every drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-read-from-a-block\"\u003EWhat is read from a block\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EConnection points:\u003C/strong\u003E attributes named like terminals (\u003Ccode\u003ETERM01\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EX1TERM02\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPIN1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECONN1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ET1\u003C/code\u003E …); without them, the ends of the straight leads that stop at the block&#039;s outline.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAttributes:\u003C/strong\u003E a tag (\u003Ccode\u003ETAG1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDEVICE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EREF\u003C/code\u003E …), a description (\u003Ccode\u003EDESC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EFUNCTION\u003C/code\u003E), a maker (\u003Ccode\u003EMFG\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EMANUFACTURER\u003C/code\u003E), a part number (\u003Ccode\u003ECAT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPART\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EMODEL\u003C/code\u003E) and an order number are mapped onto Electrical&#039;s own, with their default values. Any other attribute comes along unchanged, hidden.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe drawing stays the library&#039;s own: \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELSTANDARD\u003C/code\u003E never redraws an imported symbol.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA block without connection points can still be imported, but wires will not connect to it. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELIMPORTSYM\u003C/code\u003E says how many there were.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"If your symbols already live in a DWG or DXF drawing as blocks, you can bring them in instead of redrawing them. Type ELIMPORTSYM (or ELIS) and choose the drawing. Choose Suggested types to import every block whose name says what it is (RELAY_COIL is a coil, PB_NO a pushbutton, FUSE_2A a fuse …), or One by one to say for each block which symbol type it is, or skip it. Say whether the library is drawn in millimetres or inches. Inch libraries are scaled to millimetres, the unit Electrical draws in. Each block then acts as the type you chose: it gets a tag from the path grid, wires connect to it, coils carry the contact mirror and it shows up in the lists. Place them with ELSYMBOL › Imported. They are kept in this browser for every drawing. What is read from a block Connection points: attributes named like terminals (TERM01, X1TERM02, PIN1, CONN1, T1 …); without them, the ends of the straight leads that stop at the block's outline. Attributes: a tag (TAG1, DEVICE, REF …), a description (DESC, FUNCTION), a maker (MFG, MANUFACTURER), a part number (CAT, PART, MODEL) and an order number are mapped onto Electrical's own, with their default values. Any other attribute comes along unchanged, hidden. The drawing stays the library's own: ELSTANDARD never redraws an imported symbol. A block without connection points can still be imported, but wires will not connect to it. ELIMPORTSYM says how many there were."},{"slug":"electrical-carriers","scope":"addon:electrical","title":"Electrical: trays, conduits, cable lengths and fill","summary":"Size the routes as cable trays or conduits, type each cable's outside diameter, and get the cable schedule (with lengths along the routes) and the fill of every tray and conduit.","section":"reference","order":40,"keywords":["cable tray","conduit","fill","cable schedule","route","outside diameter","ELCARRIER","ELCR","EMT","PVC","length","slack"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe routes drawn with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELROUTE\u003C/code\u003E (lines and polylines on layers starting \u003Ccode\u003EEL-ROUTE\u003C/code\u003E) carry the circuits&#039; cables. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELCARRIER\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EELCR\u003C/code\u003E) makes them trays or conduits and judges the fill.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E: select route lines, then choose Tray (width and usable depth in mm) or Conduit (EMT or PVC40 and a trade size). The routes move to a layer that names them: \u003Ccode\u003EEL-ROUTE-TRAY-300X100\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEL-ROUTE-CONDUIT-EMT-114\u003C/code\u003E (trade size without the fraction bar: 12, 34, 1, 114, 112, 2, 212, 3, 312, 4).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOd\u003C/span\u003E: the outside diameter of a cable type, exactly as written in the circuits&#039; cable field (for example \u003Ccode\u003E3x2.5\u003C/code\u003E), from the maker. Without it the fill is not judged and the cable types are listed. It is never guessed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E: each circuit with a board is one cable, routed from the board to its farthest device along the shortest way through the routes. It prints the cable schedule (length with 10% slack and 1 m at each end), the fill of every sized tray and conduit with the next trade size that would hold the cables, and saves the schedule as CSV.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFill limits are the usual 53 / 31 / 40 % for one / two / more cables in a conduit and 50 % in a tray. They are labelled defaults; the code in force decides. A device with no continuous route from the board is listed and given no length.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The routes drawn with ELROUTE (lines and polylines on layers starting EL-ROUTE) carry the circuits' cables. ELCARRIER (ELCR) makes them trays or conduits and judges the fill. Size: select route lines, then choose Tray (width and usable depth in mm) or Conduit (EMT or PVC40 and a trade size). The routes move to a layer that names them: EL-ROUTE-TRAY-300X100, EL-ROUTE-CONDUIT-EMT-114 (trade size without the fraction bar: 12, 34, 1, 114, 112, 2, 212, 3, 312, 4). Od: the outside diameter of a cable type, exactly as written in the circuits' cable field (for example 3x2.5), from the maker. Without it the fill is not judged and the cable types are listed. It is never guessed. Run: each circuit with a board is one cable, routed from the board to its farthest device along the shortest way through the routes. It prints the cable schedule (length with 10% slack and 1 m at each end), the fill of every sized tray and conduit with the next trade size that would hold the cables, and saves the schedule as CSV. Fill limits are the usual 53 / 31 / 40 % for one / two / more cables in a conduit and 50 % in a tray. They are labelled defaults; the code in force decides. A device with no continuous route from the board is listed and given no length."},{"slug":"electrical-pv-oneline","scope":"addon:electrical","title":"Electrical: a PV one-line from BimCraft","summary":"Insert the one-line diagram BimCraft makes from its Solar PV pane (array, combiner, disconnects, inverter, board, utility) on a new sheet, with each wire's current, size and fuse.","section":"reference","order":41,"keywords":["pv","solar","one-line","single line","inverter","array","combiner","disconnect","ELPVONELINE","ELPV","photovoltaic"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EIn BimCraft, the Solar PV pane&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave data\u003C/span\u003E writes a file of the one-line. Here, type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELPVONELINE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EELPV\u003C/code\u003E) and pick it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA new A3 sheet gets the parts left to right (a PV array, a combiner when there are three or more strings, a disconnect, the inverter, another disconnect, the board and the utility), with a wire for each link labelled with its current, wire size and fuse or breaker. The symbols are in the new \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPower and solar\u003C/span\u003E group of \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELSYMBOL\u003C/code\u003E too. Run \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELTAG\u003C/code\u003E to number the parts.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sheet carries a line saying the factors and wire tables are examples set in BimCraft by the person who made the plan. It is not a compliance statement.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"In BimCraft, the Solar PV pane's Save data writes a file of the one-line. Here, type ELPVONELINE (ELPV) and pick it. A new A3 sheet gets the parts left to right (a PV array, a combiner when there are three or more strings, a disconnect, the inverter, another disconnect, the board and the utility), with a wire for each link labelled with its current, wire size and fuse or breaker. The symbols are in the new Power and solar group of ELSYMBOL too. Run ELTAG to number the parts. The sheet carries a line saying the factors and wire tables are examples set in BimCraft by the person who made the plan. It is not a compliance statement."},{"slug":"electrical-symbols","scope":"addon:electrical","title":"Electrical symbols","summary":"The symbols Electrical draws in the IEC 60617 set and the ANSI / NFPA 79 set, what each connection is called, and how to switch sets.","section":"reference","order":60,"keywords":["electrical","symbols","iec 60617","ansi","jic","nfpa 79","elstandard","contact","coil","fuse","breaker","rcd","rcbo","residual current","motor","lamp","terminal","plc","earth","transformer","block","attributes"],"headings":[{"id":"two-sets","text":"Two sets","level":2},{"id":"the-symbols","text":"The symbols","level":2},{"id":"what-a-symbol-is-in-the-file","text":"What a symbol is in the file","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"two-sets\"\u003ETwo sets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENew symbols use the set chosen in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElectrical\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESymbols\u003C/span\u003E, or with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELSTANDARD\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Cstrong\u003EIEC 60617\u003C/strong\u003E (the default) or \u003Cstrong\u003EANSI / NFPA 79\u003C/strong\u003E (the &quot;JIC&quot; style). \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELSTANDARD\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E redraws every symbol already in the drawing in the chosen set; tags, connections and values stay.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-symbols\"\u003EThe symbols\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGroup\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESymbols (default connection names)\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EContacts\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emake contact 13-14, break contact 21-22, changeover 11-12-14, delayed make contact 67-68, three-pole main contact 1-2, 3-4, 5-6\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwitches and buttons\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Epushbutton make 13-14, pushbutton break 11-12, selector switch, manual switch, limit switch\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECoils\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Erelay or contactor coil A1-A2, timer coil on delay, timer coil off delay\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProtection and switching\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efuse, circuit breaker, three-pole breaker, motor protection breaker, thermal overload relay, disconnector, residual-current device (two or four poles), RCBO (residual current with overcurrent protection)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMotors, lamps and loads\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethree-phase motor U V W, DC motor, signal lamp X1-X2, horn, transformer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETerminals and earth\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eterminal (its number), protective earth\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPLC\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Einput and output points (with an address)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-a-symbol-is-in-the-file\"\u003EWhat a symbol is in the file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach symbol is a block named \u003Ccode\u003EEL_IEC_…\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EEL_ANSI_…\u003C/code\u003E with attributes: \u003Ccode\u003ETAG\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EFUNC\u003C/code\u003E (plant), \u003Ccode\u003ELOC\u003C/code\u003E (location), \u003Ccode\u003EDESC\u003C/code\u003E (function text), \u003Ccode\u003EMFR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPART\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EORDER\u003C/code\u003E, one per connection (\u003Ccode\u003EP1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EP2\u003C/code\u003E …, its name), \u003Ccode\u003EXREF\u003C/code\u003E on contacts and \u003Ccode\u003EXREF1\u003C/code\u003E…\u003Ccode\u003EXREF8\u003C/code\u003E on coils, \u003Ccode\u003EADDR\u003C/code\u003E on PLC points, and one invisible constant attribute \u003Ccode\u003EEL\u003C/code\u003E that says what the block is and where its connections are. Other programs see blocks with attributes; CadCraft without the add-on shows and saves them unchanged.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Two sets New symbols use the set chosen in Settings › CadCraft › Electrical › Symbols, or with ELSTANDARD: IEC 60617 (the default) or ANSI / NFPA 79 (the \"JIC\" style). ELSTANDARD C redraws every symbol already in the drawing in the chosen set; tags, connections and values stay. The symbols Group Symbols (default connection names) Contacts make contact 13-14, break contact 21-22, changeover 11-12-14, delayed make contact 67-68, three-pole main contact 1-2, 3-4, 5-6 Switches and buttons pushbutton make 13-14, pushbutton break 11-12, selector switch, manual switch, limit switch Coils relay or contactor coil A1-A2, timer coil on delay, timer coil off delay Protection and switching fuse, circuit breaker, three-pole breaker, motor protection breaker, thermal overload relay, disconnector, residual-current device (two or four poles), RCBO (residual current with overcurrent protection) Motors, lamps and loads three-phase motor U V W, DC motor, signal lamp X1-X2, horn, transformer Terminals and earth terminal (its number), protective earth PLC input and output points (with an address) What a symbol is in the file Each symbol is a block named EL_IEC_… or EL_ANSI_… with attributes: TAG, FUNC (plant), LOC (location), DESC (function text), MFR, PART, ORDER, one per connection (P1, P2 …, its name), XREF on contacts and XREF1…XREF8 on coils, ADDR on PLC points, and one invisible constant attribute EL that says what the block is and where its connections are. Other programs see blocks with attributes; CadCraft without the add-on shows and saves them unchanged."},{"slug":"electrical-not-built-yet","scope":"addon:electrical","title":"Electrical: not built yet","summary":"What the Electrical add-on does not do yet, so you know before you plan a project around it.","section":"trouble","order":90,"keywords":["electrical","not built","missing","limits","roadmap","cross-sheet","potential","plc card","cable","wire colour","cross-section"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWires as objects.\u003C/strong\u003E Wires are lines and polylines on wire layers. They do not carry their own colour or cross-section yet; use the layer name (\u003Ccode\u003EEL-WIRE-BU-1.5\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWires across sheets.\u003C/strong\u003E A potential that continues on another sheet is two nets; there is no &quot;continued on&quot; arrow that joins them yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhole PLC cards and terminal blocks with several levels.\u003C/strong\u003E PLC points are placed one by one; terminals have one level.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESymbols of your own, drawn here.\u003C/strong\u003E Blocks you draw become symbols through \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELIMPORTSYM\u003C/code\u003E (save them in a drawing first); there is no editor for a symbol&#039;s connection points yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECable cores by colour or number.\u003C/strong\u003E The cable list numbers cores in the order the mark crosses them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVoltage drop and cable sizing.\u003C/strong\u003E The panel schedule gives current and length, not the voltage drop or the cable size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EInstallation symbols in 3D.\u003C/strong\u003E Plan symbols are 2D.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERCDs in the schematic&#039;s logic.\u003C/strong\u003E A residual-current device is a symbol and a line of text on a circuit; the add-on does not check which circuits it protects, its type (A, F, B), its selectivity or its trip time. Fire detection is symbols only: no loops, addresses or zones.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOther languages.\u003C/strong\u003E The words are in a language file, but only English is written so far.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Wires as objects. Wires are lines and polylines on wire layers. They do not carry their own colour or cross-section yet; use the layer name (EL-WIRE-BU-1.5). Wires across sheets. A potential that continues on another sheet is two nets; there is no \"continued on\" arrow that joins them yet. Whole PLC cards and terminal blocks with several levels. PLC points are placed one by one; terminals have one level. Symbols of your own, drawn here. Blocks you draw become symbols through ELIMPORTSYM (save them in a drawing first); there is no editor for a symbol's connection points yet. Cable cores by colour or number. The cable list numbers cores in the order the mark crosses them. Voltage drop and cable sizing. The panel schedule gives current and length, not the voltage drop or the cable size. Installation symbols in 3D. Plan symbols are 2D. RCDs in the schematic's logic. A residual-current device is a symbol and a line of text on a circuit; the add-on does not check which circuits it protects, its type (A, F, B), its selectivity or its trip time. Fire detection is symbols only: no loops, addresses or zones. Other languages. The words are in a language file, but only English is written so far."}]};
